      "mergeID": "5b883401e4257056e3b258a0",
      "mergedPostDetails": "I have personally experimented  enhanced \"onward\" type movement and had tremendous success. Movement as directed by orientation of pointing doesn't cause nearly as much vestibular disconnect as much as directing where you are going by looking with your head (almost guaranteed for some people). \n\nYour head isn't always looking in the direction your body is moving (nor are your eyes: but we can't read that information just yet. ;) ). \n\nHowever, your forearms and upper body are almost always oriented roughly the same way, or are shortly after you start moving - which is why it works so well - it's natural.  This is further enhanced by adding in a maximum speed you can move (with no acceleration ramp!) depending on how well oriented -within a box of variance -that the x axis (y can be ignored) of the left (or right, depending on which arm is used to direct movement) forearm and right upper arm + head are. \n\nThat way:  if someone has their arm pointed and outstretched to the left at or around 90 deg; but are instead looking straight ahead with their right arm at their side - you only allow very slow movement in the direction the left hand is pointing. \n\nIf they instead turn to face the direction of movement, you first have a medium walking pace, and a click to the pad and hold is a sprint. \n\nYou can ignore all directional input from the top half from the left trackpad/stick on vive/touch and treat it as a big \"walk the way the lower arm is pointing\"  button, with click to \"sprint the way the lower arm is pointing, as long as we're also facing the same way\" . Works well. Also, you lockout changes in movement speed unless the user lets go and then touches or touch+clicks again - you again are running a challenge on the xyz translation/transform of the user avatar parts to see how much speed you're going to allow this time. NO acceleration should ever be allowed for this method to work - this is important, but an instant change in speed is fine as long as the user is the one initiating it. They're \"stepping on the gas\"\n\nI've found that this is not only the most effective form of free locomotion but the most accessible, and it falls in the upper middle to high range of comfort options. It mimics an electric wheelchair: and works for the same reason that a passenger might get carsick - but if that same passenger is instead driving they don't - it's the brain's ability to override the vestibular system and say \"I'm choosing to do this\" that prevents the simsickness for a lot of people.",
      "mergedPostTitle": "Forearm orientation hinted movement",
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "59d80f927cd2f35784aa7599",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "value": "I'm having a hard time imagining this. Especially because I haven't played neither onward nor vrchat in vr (because I don't have a headset) but seeing how much detail you put into this and some details that I can understand/relate to. I'll up it :)",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
